1
00:00:07,020 --> 00:00:14,050
Listen, practice, speak, practice makes you perfect.

2
00:00:14,050 --> 00:00:17,050
Hello and welcome to Business English Conversations.

3
00:00:17,050 --> 00:00:18,050
Hello, everyone.

4
00:00:18,050 --> 00:00:22,050
Welcome back to the podcast where we practice real English for the workplace.

5
00:00:22,050 --> 00:00:23,050
My name is Emma.

6
00:00:23,050 --> 00:00:24,050
And my name is David.

7
00:00:24,050 --> 00:00:30,050
In this podcast, we help you learn how to communicate more confidently in professional situations.

8
00:00:30,050 --> 00:00:35,050
Yes, especially situations that many people face at work, like meetings, presentations,

9
00:00:35,050 --> 00:00:38,130
interviews, and everyday conversations with colleagues.

10
00:00:38,130 --> 00:00:40,610
Today we have a very useful topic.

11
00:00:40,610 --> 00:00:44,930
Our topic today is how to explain a project you worked on in English.

12
00:00:44,930 --> 00:00:49,210
This is an important skill because at work, people often ask about your projects.

13
00:00:49,210 --> 00:00:53,570
For example, during a meeting, a performance review, or even a job interview.

14
00:00:53,570 --> 00:00:55,090
Someone might ask questions like,

15
00:00:55,090 --> 00:00:57,130
Can you tell us about a project you worked on?

16
00:00:57,130 --> 00:00:58,890
What was your role in the project?

17
00:00:58,890 --> 00:01:00,690
What challenges did you face?

18
00:01:00,690 --> 00:01:02,930
And what were the results of the project?

19
00:01:02,930 --> 00:01:07,850
In today's conversation, we will show you how to explain a project clearly and confidently.

20
00:01:07,850 --> 00:01:13,330
We will talk about the background, the goal, the process, the challenges, and the results.

21
00:01:13,330 --> 00:01:16,130
And we will use simple and natural business English.

22
00:01:16,130 --> 00:01:17,290
So let's begin.

23
00:01:17,290 --> 00:01:20,330
Emma, have you worked on any interesting projects recently?

24
00:01:20,330 --> 00:01:22,090
Yes, actually I have.

25
00:01:22,090 --> 00:01:25,810
A few months ago, I worked on a very interesting project at my company.

26
00:01:25,810 --> 00:01:26,810
That sounds great.

27
00:01:26,810 --> 00:01:27,970
Can you tell me about it?

28
00:01:27,970 --> 00:01:28,970
Of course.

29
00:01:28,970 --> 00:01:32,890
The project was about improving our company's customer support system.

30
00:01:32,890 --> 00:01:34,690
That sounds like an important project.

31
00:01:34,690 --> 00:01:36,090
Yes, it was.

32
00:01:36,090 --> 00:01:39,730
Many customers had complained that our response time was too slow.

33
00:01:39,730 --> 00:01:42,170
So the company decided to make some changes.

34
00:01:42,170 --> 00:01:43,170
Exactly.

35
00:01:43,170 --> 00:01:47,930
Our management team wanted to create a faster and more efficient support process.

36
00:01:47,930 --> 00:01:49,930
And what was your role in this project?

37
00:01:49,930 --> 00:01:52,050
I was the project coordinator.

38
00:01:52,050 --> 00:01:53,330
What did that involve?

39
00:01:53,330 --> 00:01:58,130
My main responsibility was to organize the project timeline and coordinate communication

40
00:01:58,130 --> 00:01:59,650
between different teams.

41
00:01:59,650 --> 00:02:01,970
Which teams were involved in the project?

42
00:02:01,970 --> 00:02:07,650
teams participated. We had the customer service team, the IT department, and the data analysis

43
00:02:07,650 --> 00:02:08,650
team.

44
00:02:08,650 --> 00:02:10,170
That sounds like a large collaboration.

45
00:02:10,170 --> 00:02:12,970
Yes, it was definitely a team effort.

46
00:02:12,970 --> 00:02:14,450
When did the project start?

47
00:02:14,450 --> 00:02:17,770
The project started in January and lasted about four months.

48
00:02:17,770 --> 00:02:19,690
That's a fairly long project.

49
00:02:19,690 --> 00:02:25,450
Yes, because we wanted to carefully analyze the problem before implementing solutions.

50
00:02:25,450 --> 00:02:27,770
That's very important in project management.

51
00:02:27,770 --> 00:02:32,510
Absolutely. If you rush the planning stage, you might create more problems later.

52
00:02:32,510 --> 00:02:34,310
So how did the project begin?

53
00:02:34,310 --> 00:02:38,110
First, we collected data about our customer service performance.

54
00:02:38,110 --> 00:02:39,970
What kind of data did you analyze?

55
00:02:39,970 --> 00:02:44,310
We looked at response times, customer satisfaction scores, and the number of support tickets

56
00:02:44,310 --> 00:02:45,310
per day.

57
00:02:45,310 --> 00:02:46,750
What did the data reveal?

58
00:02:46,750 --> 00:02:51,230
The data showed that our average response time was nearly 24 hours.

59
00:02:51,230 --> 00:02:53,550
That is quite slow for customer support.

60
00:02:53,550 --> 00:02:57,550
Yes, especially in today's fast-paced digital world.

61
00:02:57,550 --> 00:02:59,510
customers expect quick responses.

62
00:02:59,510 --> 00:03:00,670
Exactly.

63
00:03:00,670 --> 00:03:04,070
Many customers expect replies within just a few hours.

64
00:03:04,070 --> 00:03:06,710
So what was the main goal of your project?

65
00:03:06,710 --> 00:03:11,270
Our main goal was to reduce the average response time to under six hours.

66
00:03:11,270 --> 00:03:12,710
That's a big improvement.

67
00:03:12,710 --> 00:03:16,270
Yes, it was ambitious, but we believed it was achievable.

68
00:03:16,270 --> 00:03:18,750
What steps did your team take to reach that goal?

69
00:03:18,750 --> 00:03:22,110
First, we identified the main reasons for the delays.

70
00:03:22,110 --> 00:03:24,010
What were the biggest problems?

71
00:03:24,010 --> 00:03:28,490
One major issue was that customer inquiries were not categorized properly.

72
00:03:28,490 --> 00:03:30,130
What do you mean by that?

73
00:03:30,130 --> 00:03:32,690
All tickets were going into the same queue.

74
00:03:32,690 --> 00:03:36,330
So simple questions and complex problems were mixed together?

75
00:03:36,330 --> 00:03:37,330
Exactly.

76
00:03:37,330 --> 00:03:40,570
This created confusion and slowed down the process.

77
00:03:40,570 --> 00:03:41,890
That makes sense.

78
00:03:41,890 --> 00:03:46,450
Another problem was that the customer service team didn't have enough automation tools.

79
00:03:46,450 --> 00:03:48,730
So many tasks had to be done manually?

80
00:03:48,730 --> 00:03:49,730
Yes.

81
00:03:49,730 --> 00:03:51,450
Which made the process inefficient?

82
00:03:51,450 --> 00:03:53,370
What solution did your team propose?

83
00:03:53,370 --> 00:03:56,890
We proposed implementing a new ticket management system.

84
00:03:56,890 --> 00:03:58,210
What would that system do?

85
00:03:58,210 --> 00:04:02,290
It would automatically categorize customer inquiries based on keywords.

86
00:04:02,290 --> 00:04:03,570
That sounds very helpful.

87
00:04:03,570 --> 00:04:06,970
Yes, it allowed urgent issues to be prioritized.

88
00:04:06,970 --> 00:04:08,970
Who worked on building the new system?

89
00:04:08,970 --> 00:04:12,110
The IT team developed and configured the system.

90
00:04:12,110 --> 00:04:14,050
And what was your role during that stage?

91
00:04:14,050 --> 00:04:18,290
I coordinated meetings between the IT team and the customer service team.

92
00:04:18,290 --> 00:04:19,810
Why was that important?

93
00:04:19,810 --> 00:04:24,250
Because the IT team needed to understand the real needs of the support staff.

94
00:04:24,250 --> 00:04:26,490
Communication between teams is always critical.

95
00:04:26,490 --> 00:04:27,650
Absolutely.

96
00:04:27,650 --> 00:04:29,850
Were there any challenges during the project?

97
00:04:29,850 --> 00:04:31,570
Yes, several.

98
00:04:31,570 --> 00:04:33,250
What was the biggest challenge?

99
00:04:33,250 --> 00:04:35,910
One challenge was resistance to change.

100
00:04:35,910 --> 00:04:38,050
That happens in many organizations.

101
00:04:38,050 --> 00:04:41,530
Yes, some employees were comfortable with the old system.

102
00:04:41,530 --> 00:04:43,930
They were worried about learning a new system.

103
00:04:43,930 --> 00:04:44,930
Exactly.

104
00:04:44,930 --> 00:04:46,690
How did you handle that challenge?

105
00:04:46,690 --> 00:04:50,010
We organized training sessions for the customer service team.

106
00:04:50,010 --> 00:04:51,850
What kind of training did you provide?

107
00:04:51,850 --> 00:04:55,370
We showed them how the new system worked and explained the benefits.

108
00:04:55,370 --> 00:04:57,330
Did that help reduce their concerns?

109
00:04:57,330 --> 00:04:59,330
Yes, gradually.

110
00:04:59,330 --> 00:05:02,690
People usually feel more comfortable once they understand the benefits.

111
00:05:02,690 --> 00:05:03,690
That's true.

112
00:05:03,690 --> 00:05:05,450
Were there any technical challenges?

113
00:05:05,450 --> 00:05:06,610
Yes.

114
00:05:06,610 --> 00:05:09,930
Integrating the new system with our existing database was complicated.

115
00:05:09,930 --> 00:05:11,570
I can imagine.

116
00:05:11,570 --> 00:05:15,090
The IT team had to spend extra time testing the integration.

117
00:05:15,090 --> 00:05:17,490
Did the project timeline change because of that?

118
00:05:17,490 --> 00:05:20,450
Yes, we had a slight delay of about two weeks.

119
00:05:20,450 --> 00:05:22,970
That's not too bad for a technical project.

120
00:05:22,970 --> 00:05:25,690
Fortunately, management was very supportive.

121
00:05:25,690 --> 00:05:26,890
That always helps.

122
00:05:26,890 --> 00:05:30,410
They understood that quality was more important than speed.

123
00:05:30,410 --> 00:05:33,250
So after the system was ready, what happened next?

124
00:05:33,250 --> 00:05:35,450
We launched a pilot program.

125
00:05:35,450 --> 00:05:37,050
What is a pilot program?

126
00:05:37,050 --> 00:05:40,930
It means testing the new system with a small group before full implementation.

127
00:05:40,930 --> 00:05:42,690
That's a smart approach.

128
00:05:42,690 --> 00:05:45,530
Yes, it allows you to identify problems early.

129
00:05:45,530 --> 00:05:47,690
Which team participated in the pilot?

130
00:05:47,690 --> 00:05:50,210
A small group from the customer service department.

131
00:05:50,210 --> 00:05:52,290
How long did the pilot phase last?

132
00:05:52,290 --> 00:05:53,930
About three weeks.

133
00:05:53,930 --> 00:05:55,770
What did you learn during that time?

134
00:05:55,770 --> 00:05:59,450
We discovered some small issues with ticket categorization.

135
00:05:59,450 --> 00:06:01,570
Were those issues easy to fix?

136
00:06:01,570 --> 00:06:04,570
Yes, the IT team adjusted the system settings.

137
00:06:04,570 --> 00:06:08,010
After the pilot program, did you implement the system company-wide?

138
00:06:08,010 --> 00:06:11,610
Yes, we rolled it out to the entire customer support department.

139
00:06:11,610 --> 00:06:13,210
How did employees react?

140
00:06:13,210 --> 00:06:17,610
At first they were cautious, but soon they started appreciating the improvements.

141
00:06:17,610 --> 00:06:19,610
What kind of improvements did they notice?

142
00:06:19,610 --> 00:06:23,210
The system reduced their workload and helped them respond faster.

143
00:06:23,210 --> 00:06:25,210
That must have been satisfying for the team.

144
00:06:25,210 --> 00:06:27,810
Yes, everyone felt proud of the results.

145
00:06:27,810 --> 00:06:31,410
Speaking of results, what were the final outcomes of the project?

146
00:06:31,410 --> 00:06:33,410
The results were very positive.

147
00:06:33,410 --> 00:06:34,910
Can you share some numbers?

148
00:06:34,910 --> 00:06:38,910
Before the project, our average response time was 24 hours.

149
00:06:38,910 --> 00:06:40,610
And after the project?

150
00:06:40,610 --> 00:06:43,010
It decreased to about five hours.

151
00:06:43,010 --> 00:06:44,690
That's a huge improvement.

152
00:06:44,690 --> 00:06:47,250
Yes, we exceeded our original goal.

153
00:06:47,250 --> 00:06:49,170
What about customer satisfaction?

154
00:06:49,170 --> 00:06:52,210
Customer satisfaction scores increased by nearly 30%.

155
00:06:52,930 --> 00:06:54,210
That's excellent.

156
00:06:54,210 --> 00:06:57,090
Yes, the management team was very pleased.

157
00:06:57,090 --> 00:06:59,890
Did the company plan any follow-up improvements?

158
00:06:59,890 --> 00:07:04,450
Yes, we're now exploring the use of AI chatbots for basic questions.

159
00:07:04,450 --> 00:07:06,930
That could improve response times even further.

160
00:07:06,930 --> 00:07:07,970
Exactly.

161
00:07:07,970 --> 00:07:11,730
Looking back, what was the most valuable lesson from the project?

162
00:07:11,730 --> 00:07:15,490
I learned that communication is the key to successful teamwork.

163
00:07:15,490 --> 00:07:16,930
That's a great lesson.

164
00:07:16,930 --> 00:07:19,810
Without clear communication, the project could have failed.

165
00:07:19,810 --> 00:07:21,330
I completely agree.

166
00:07:21,330 --> 00:07:26,050
Also, involving employees early in the process helped reduce resistance.

167
00:07:26,050 --> 00:07:27,730
That's a very important point.

168
00:07:27,730 --> 00:07:30,130
People are more supportive when they feel included.

169
00:07:30,130 --> 00:07:33,250
If you had to do the project again, would you change anything?

170
00:07:33,250 --> 00:07:36,530
I would probably spend more time in the planning stage.

171
00:07:36,530 --> 00:07:37,490
Why is that?

172
00:07:37,490 --> 00:07:40,930
because better planning could have prevented some technical issues.

173
00:07:40,930 --> 00:07:41,970
That makes sense.

174
00:07:41,970 --> 00:07:44,850
But overall, I think the project was very successful.

175
00:07:44,850 --> 00:07:46,450
It certainly sounds like it.

176
00:07:46,450 --> 00:07:49,890
Emma, have you ever had to explain a project during a job interview?

177
00:07:49,890 --> 00:07:51,490
Yes, many times.

178
00:07:51,490 --> 00:07:53,250
What kind of questions do they ask?

179
00:07:53,250 --> 00:07:57,090
Interviewers often asked me to describe a project and explain my role.

180
00:07:57,090 --> 00:07:58,410
That's very common.

181
00:07:58,410 --> 00:08:01,410
They also wanted to know about challenges and results.

182
00:08:01,410 --> 00:08:04,890
That's why it's useful to organize your explanation in a clear structure.

183
00:08:04,890 --> 00:08:06,130
Exactly.

184
00:08:06,130 --> 00:08:08,690
First, explain the background of the project.

185
00:08:08,690 --> 00:08:10,450
Second, describe the goal.

186
00:08:10,450 --> 00:08:13,210
Third, explain your role and responsibilities.

187
00:08:13,210 --> 00:08:16,170
Fourth, talk about the challenges and how you solve them.

188
00:08:16,170 --> 00:08:18,890
And finally, share the results and what you learned.

189
00:08:18,890 --> 00:08:20,410
This structure helps your explanation

190
00:08:20,410 --> 00:08:22,250
sound clear and professional.

191
00:08:22,250 --> 00:08:25,330
It also helps the listener understand the full story.

192
00:08:25,330 --> 00:08:27,730
And that is exactly what employers want to hear.

193
00:08:27,730 --> 00:08:30,730
Before we finish today, we would like to ask you a question.

194
00:08:30,730 --> 00:08:32,850
Yes, here's today's question for you.

195
00:08:32,850 --> 00:08:35,290
Can you describe a project you worked on?

196
00:08:35,290 --> 00:08:39,370
It can be a work project, a school project, or even a personal project.

197
00:08:39,370 --> 00:08:41,450
Try to answer using this structure.

198
00:08:41,450 --> 00:08:43,050
What was the goal of the project?

199
00:08:43,050 --> 00:08:44,370
What was your role?

200
00:08:44,370 --> 00:08:46,250
What challenges did you face?

201
00:08:46,250 --> 00:08:48,010
And what were the results?

202
00:08:48,010 --> 00:08:50,730
Practicing this will help you improve your business English.

203
00:08:50,730 --> 00:08:52,430
Thank you so much for listening today.

204
00:08:52,430 --> 00:08:57,210
We hope this conversation helps you feel more confident when talking about your work experience.

205
00:08:57,210 --> 00:08:59,250
Keep practicing your English every day.

206
00:08:59,250 --> 00:09:03,690
And remember, communication is one of the most important skills in business.

207
00:09:03,690 --> 00:09:04,690
From both of us.

208
00:09:04,690 --> 00:09:05,810
Goodbye, everyone.

209
00:09:05,810 --> 00:09:06,810
See you next time.

210
00:09:06,810 --> 00:09:10,690
Hello, everyone, and welcome back to our English Learning Podcast.

211
00:09:10,690 --> 00:09:13,810
If you're joining us for the first time, thank you for being here.

212
00:09:13,810 --> 00:09:18,850
This podcast is designed to help you improve your English through natural conversations

213
00:09:18,850 --> 00:09:22,250
about real-life professional situations.

214
00:09:22,250 --> 00:09:23,250
That's right.

215
00:09:23,250 --> 00:09:28,690
Our goal is to help you become more confident when speaking English at work, especially

216
00:09:28,690 --> 00:09:32,970
in meetings, presentations, and professional discussions.

217
00:09:32,970 --> 00:09:36,930
I'm Speaker 2, and I'm here with my colleague, Speaker 1.

218
00:09:36,930 --> 00:09:41,930
Thanks. Today we're going to talk about something extremely useful in the workplace.

219
00:09:41,930 --> 00:09:45,410
How to explain a project you worked on in English.

220
00:09:45,410 --> 00:09:48,490
Yes, this is a very important skill.

221
00:09:48,490 --> 00:09:53,010
Whether you are in a job interview, a team meeting, a performance review,

222
00:09:53,010 --> 00:09:56,010
or even a casual conversation with colleagues,

223
00:09:56,010 --> 00:09:59,490
you will often need to describe a project you worked on.

224
00:09:59,490 --> 00:10:05,410
Exactly. Many professionals know their work very well, but sometimes they struggle to explain it

225
00:10:05,410 --> 00:10:10,370
clearly in English. They may have the technical knowledge, but organizing their explanation

226
00:10:10,370 --> 00:10:16,370
can be difficult. So today, we're going to simulate a realistic conversation where one

227
00:10:16,370 --> 00:10:21,810
professional explains a project they worked on. During the conversation, we'll naturally

228
00:10:21,810 --> 00:10:27,810
demonstrate useful vocabulary, phrases, and structures you can use in your own workplace

229
00:10:27,810 --> 00:10:29,150
conversations.

230
00:10:29,150 --> 00:10:30,890
Imagine this situation.

231
00:10:30,890 --> 00:10:33,810
Two colleagues are discussing recent projects

232
00:10:33,810 --> 00:10:35,250
during a weekly meeting.

233
00:10:35,250 --> 00:10:38,450
Speaker 2 asks Speaker 1 about a project

234
00:10:38,450 --> 00:10:40,050
they recently completed.

235
00:10:40,050 --> 00:10:42,490
All right, let's begin the conversation.

236
00:10:42,490 --> 00:10:44,930
So during the last team meeting,

237
00:10:44,930 --> 00:10:47,650
you briefly mentioned that you had just completed

238
00:10:47,650 --> 00:10:50,690
a major project with the operations department.

239
00:10:50,690 --> 00:10:52,410
It sounded really interesting,

240
00:10:52,410 --> 00:10:55,530
but we didn't have enough time to go into details.

241
00:10:55,530 --> 00:10:57,370
I'd love to hear more about it.

242
00:10:57,370 --> 00:10:59,370
Could you walk me through the project?

243
00:10:59,370 --> 00:11:01,050
Sure, I'd be happy to.

244
00:11:01,050 --> 00:11:03,730
It was actually one of the most challenging projects

245
00:11:03,730 --> 00:11:04,910
I've worked on this year,

246
00:11:04,910 --> 00:11:07,210
but also one of the most rewarding.

247
00:11:07,210 --> 00:11:10,610
The project focused on improving the internal workflow

248
00:11:10,610 --> 00:11:12,730
of our order processing system.

249
00:11:12,730 --> 00:11:14,310
That sounds important.

250
00:11:14,310 --> 00:11:17,290
What was the main problem you were trying to solve?

251
00:11:17,290 --> 00:11:20,690
Well, the main issue was that our order processing system

252
00:11:20,690 --> 00:11:23,050
had become inefficient over time.

253
00:11:23,050 --> 00:11:24,210
As the company grew,

254
00:11:24,210 --> 00:11:26,170
in the number of customers increased,

255
00:11:26,170 --> 00:11:29,790
the system we were using couldn't handle the volume efficiently.

256
00:11:29,790 --> 00:11:31,770
Orders were sometimes delayed,

257
00:11:31,770 --> 00:11:36,590
and the operations team had to spend a lot of time manually correcting errors.

258
00:11:36,590 --> 00:11:41,290
I see. So the project was about making the process more efficient.

259
00:11:41,290 --> 00:11:44,610
Exactly. The goal was to streamline the workflow,

260
00:11:44,610 --> 00:11:48,650
reduce manual work, and improve overall processing speed.

261
00:11:48,650 --> 00:11:51,810
Management wanted us to analyze the existing system

262
00:11:51,810 --> 00:11:55,610
and propose improvements that could save time and reduce errors.

263
00:11:55,610 --> 00:11:58,010
That sounds like a complex task.

264
00:11:58,010 --> 00:11:59,810
How did the project start?

265
00:11:59,810 --> 00:12:02,210
The project started with a planning phase.

266
00:12:02,210 --> 00:12:04,730
First, our team met with several departments,

267
00:12:04,730 --> 00:12:08,410
including operations, IT, and customer service.

268
00:12:08,410 --> 00:12:11,370
We wanted to understand how each department interacted

269
00:12:11,370 --> 00:12:13,250
with the order processing system.

270
00:12:13,250 --> 00:12:14,410
That makes sense.

271
00:12:14,410 --> 00:12:16,730
You needed to see the entire process

272
00:12:16,730 --> 00:12:18,450
before making changes.

273
00:12:18,450 --> 00:12:19,450
Exactly.

274
00:12:19,450 --> 00:12:21,770
During those meetings, we asked many questions

275
00:12:21,770 --> 00:12:23,330
about daily workflows.

276
00:12:23,330 --> 00:12:30,130
We also collected data on processing times, error rates, and customer complaints related to order delays.

277
00:12:30,130 --> 00:12:35,330
So you were basically gathering information and identifying the main pain points?

278
00:12:35,330 --> 00:12:43,830
Yes, that's right. Once we gathered enough information, we created a process map that showed every step of the order processing workflow.

279
00:12:43,830 --> 00:12:47,130
A process map? Could you explain what that is?

280
00:12:47,130 --> 00:12:53,130
Of course, a process map is a visual diagram that shows how a process works from beginning

281
00:12:53,130 --> 00:12:54,130
to end.

282
00:12:54,130 --> 00:12:58,330
It includes each step the people involved in the systems used.

283
00:12:58,330 --> 00:13:02,170
That sounds very helpful for understanding the big picture.

284
00:13:02,170 --> 00:13:03,630
It definitely was.

285
00:13:03,630 --> 00:13:06,930
When we mapped everything out, we noticed several bottlenecks.

286
00:13:06,930 --> 00:13:11,690
For example, certain approvals required manual verification, which slowed down

287
00:13:11,690 --> 00:13:13,650
the process significantly.

288
00:13:13,650 --> 00:13:18,770
Interesting, so once you identified the bottlenecks, what was the next step?

289
00:13:18,770 --> 00:13:23,210
After identifying the issues, our team moved into the solution design phase.

290
00:13:23,210 --> 00:13:26,610
We brainstormed several ways to improve the workflow.

291
00:13:26,610 --> 00:13:31,890
Some ideas involved automation, while others focused on simplifying certain procedures.

292
00:13:31,890 --> 00:13:35,290
Did you work closely with the IT department during this phase?

293
00:13:35,290 --> 00:13:37,090
Yes, very closely.

294
00:13:37,090 --> 00:13:42,770
Since many of the proposed improvements involved software changes, IT played a crucial role.

295
00:13:42,770 --> 00:13:48,330
We had weekly meetings to discuss feasibility, development timelines, and system integration.

296
00:13:48,330 --> 00:13:52,530
That kind of collaboration is essential for a project like this.

297
00:13:52,530 --> 00:13:53,530
Absolutely.

298
00:13:53,530 --> 00:13:58,370
Without strong communication between teams, the project would have been much more difficult.

299
00:13:58,370 --> 00:14:02,130
I'm curious, what was your specific role in the project?

300
00:14:02,130 --> 00:14:06,690
My main responsibility was project coordination in process analysis.

301
00:14:06,690 --> 00:14:10,490
I worked closely with stakeholders from different departments to ensure that their

302
00:14:10,490 --> 00:14:14,890
requirements were understood and incorporated into the final solution.

303
00:14:14,890 --> 00:14:19,770
So you were acting as a bridge between the technical team and the business teams.

304
00:14:19,770 --> 00:14:20,770
Exactly.

305
00:14:20,770 --> 00:14:26,370
I had to translate business needs into technical requirements and also explain technical limitations

306
00:14:26,370 --> 00:14:28,770
to non-technical stakeholders.

307
00:14:28,770 --> 00:14:30,810
That can be challenging sometimes.

308
00:14:30,810 --> 00:14:32,370
It definitely can.

309
00:14:32,370 --> 00:14:36,610
One of the biggest challenges was aligning everyone's expectations.

310
00:14:36,610 --> 00:14:41,850
departments had different priorities and sometimes their requests conflicted with each other.

311
00:14:41,850 --> 00:14:44,070
How did you handle that situation?

312
00:14:44,070 --> 00:14:48,530
We organized several alignment meetings where representatives from each department could

313
00:14:48,530 --> 00:14:50,890
discuss their concerns openly.

314
00:14:50,890 --> 00:14:54,930
During those meetings, we focused on finding solutions that would benefit the overall

315
00:14:54,930 --> 00:14:57,930
process rather than just one department.

316
00:14:57,930 --> 00:15:00,490
That sounds like a very practical approach.

317
00:15:00,490 --> 00:15:03,790
Yes, and it helped build trust among the teams.

318
00:15:03,790 --> 00:15:08,070
felt that their voices were being heard, which made collaboration easier.

319
00:15:08,070 --> 00:15:11,310
Once the solution was designed, what happened next?

320
00:15:11,310 --> 00:15:14,070
The next phase was development and testing.

321
00:15:14,070 --> 00:15:18,990
The IT team began developing the new automated features, while our team prepared the testing

322
00:15:18,990 --> 00:15:20,270
scenarios.

323
00:15:20,270 --> 00:15:22,350
What kind of testing did you conduct?

324
00:15:22,350 --> 00:15:24,630
We conducted several rounds of testing.

325
00:15:24,630 --> 00:15:28,110
First, there was internal testing by the developers.

326
00:15:28,110 --> 00:15:32,630
After that, we conducted user acceptance testing, where employees from the operations

327
00:15:32,630 --> 00:15:36,070
department used the system and provided feedback.

328
00:15:36,070 --> 00:15:39,070
User feedback is very valuable at that stage.

329
00:15:39,070 --> 00:15:40,070
Absolutely.

330
00:15:40,070 --> 00:15:45,390
In fact, we discovered several minor issues during user testing that we hadn't anticipated.

331
00:15:45,390 --> 00:15:51,070
For example, some screens were not very intuitive, so we had to redesign them to improve usability.

332
00:15:51,070 --> 00:15:55,150
That's a great example of why testing is so important.

333
00:15:55,150 --> 00:15:56,150
Exactly.

334
00:15:56,150 --> 00:16:00,550
Testing helps ensure that the final product actually meets users' needs.

335
00:16:00,550 --> 00:16:04,390
How long did the entire project take from start to finish?

336
00:16:04,390 --> 00:16:06,830
The project took approximately four months.

337
00:16:06,830 --> 00:16:09,990
The planning and analysis phase lasted about one month.

338
00:16:09,990 --> 00:16:14,270
The development phase took two months, and the final month was dedicated to testing and

339
00:16:14,270 --> 00:16:15,270
implementation.

340
00:16:15,270 --> 00:16:17,630
That's quite a significant project.

341
00:16:17,630 --> 00:16:20,870
Yes, but the results were definitely worth the effort.

342
00:16:20,870 --> 00:16:23,350
I was just about to ask about the results.

343
00:16:23,350 --> 00:16:25,910
What were the final results of the project?

344
00:16:25,910 --> 00:16:30,070
After implementing the new system, we saw several measurable improvements.

345
00:16:30,070 --> 00:16:35,750
After processing time decreased by about 30% and the number of manual corrections dropped

346
00:16:35,750 --> 00:16:36,750
significantly.

347
00:16:36,750 --> 00:16:38,250
That's impressive!

348
00:16:38,250 --> 00:16:42,750
Yes, and customer satisfaction also improved because orders were delivered more quickly

349
00:16:42,750 --> 00:16:44,010
and accurately.

350
00:16:44,010 --> 00:16:50,390
It sounds like the project had a strong impact on both internal efficiency and customer experience.

351
00:16:50,390 --> 00:16:51,390
Exactly.

352
00:16:51,390 --> 00:16:53,950
That was one of the main goals from the beginning.

353
00:16:53,950 --> 00:16:57,830
Looking back at the project, what would you say was the most valuable lesson you

354
00:16:57,830 --> 00:16:58,830
learned?

355
00:16:58,830 --> 00:17:02,970
I would say the most valuable lesson was the importance of communication.

356
00:17:02,970 --> 00:17:07,530
When multiple departments are involved in a project, clear and consistent communication

357
00:17:07,530 --> 00:17:09,130
becomes essential.

358
00:17:09,130 --> 00:17:10,530
That's very true.

359
00:17:10,530 --> 00:17:13,730
Miscommunication can easily slow down a project.

360
00:17:13,730 --> 00:17:19,230
Yes, and another lesson was the importance of involving end users early in the process.

361
00:17:19,230 --> 00:17:22,930
Their feedback helped us design a system that truly met their needs.

362
00:17:22,930 --> 00:17:27,370
If you had the chance to do the project again, would you do anything differently?

363
00:17:27,370 --> 00:17:28,530
That's a good question.

364
00:17:28,530 --> 00:17:32,490
I think I would involve the training team earlier in the process.

365
00:17:32,490 --> 00:17:37,050
When the system was finally implemented, some employees needed additional training to adapt

366
00:17:37,050 --> 00:17:38,050
to the new workflow.

367
00:17:38,050 --> 00:17:40,330
That's an interesting point.

368
00:17:40,330 --> 00:17:44,130
Change management is often overlooked in technical projects.

369
00:17:44,130 --> 00:17:45,130
Exactly.

370
00:17:45,130 --> 00:17:49,650
Even if a new system is technically better, people still need time and support to adjust

371
00:17:49,650 --> 00:17:50,650
to it.

372
00:17:50,650 --> 00:17:53,570
Overall, it sounds like the project was very successful.

373
00:17:53,570 --> 00:17:57,930
Yes, it was a great experience and a valuable opportunity to collaborate with different

374
00:17:57,930 --> 00:17:59,730
departments across the company.

375
00:17:59,730 --> 00:18:01,250
Thank you for sharing that.

376
00:18:01,250 --> 00:18:05,050
I think many professionals will find this example very helpful

377
00:18:05,050 --> 00:18:08,530
when they need to explain a project they worked on in English.

378
00:18:08,530 --> 00:18:09,370
I hope so.

379
00:18:09,370 --> 00:18:11,370
When explaining a project, it's helpful

380
00:18:11,370 --> 00:18:13,250
to follow a clear structure.

381
00:18:13,250 --> 00:18:16,370
You can start by describing the background or problem,

382
00:18:16,370 --> 00:18:19,290
then explain the goal of the project, your role,

383
00:18:19,290 --> 00:18:21,850
the steps you took, the challenges you faced,

384
00:18:21,850 --> 00:18:23,610
and finally the results.

385
00:18:23,610 --> 00:18:26,530
That structure makes your explanation much easier

386
00:18:26,530 --> 00:18:28,050
for listeners to follow.

387
00:18:28,050 --> 00:18:28,810
Exactly.

388
00:18:28,810 --> 00:18:31,250
It helps organize your thoughts and ensures

389
00:18:31,250 --> 00:18:33,610
that you cover all the important points.

390
00:18:33,610 --> 00:18:36,810
Well, that brings us to the end of today's conversation.

391
00:18:36,810 --> 00:18:38,290
Thank you for listening, everyone.

392
00:18:38,290 --> 00:18:40,810
We hope this discussion helps you feel more confident

393
00:18:40,810 --> 00:18:43,610
when explaining your professional projects in English.

394
00:18:43,610 --> 00:18:46,090
And remember, the more you practice speaking

395
00:18:46,090 --> 00:18:48,250
about your professional experiences,

396
00:18:48,250 --> 00:18:51,450
the more natural and fluent your English will become.

397
00:18:51,450 --> 00:18:53,170
We'll see you again in the next episode

398
00:18:53,170 --> 00:18:55,930
with another Real World English Conversation.

399
00:18:55,930 --> 00:19:00,010
Until then, keep practicing and keep improving your English.

400
00:19:00,010 --> 00:19:03,890
Hello, everyone, and welcome back to our English Learning Podcast.

401
00:19:03,890 --> 00:19:09,710
Today, we are going to talk about something extremely useful for students, professionals,

402
00:19:09,710 --> 00:19:11,610
and anyone learning English.

403
00:19:11,610 --> 00:19:16,050
The topic is how to explain a project you worked on in English.

404
00:19:16,050 --> 00:19:18,710
This situation happens very often.

405
00:19:18,710 --> 00:19:22,750
Maybe you are in a job interview and the interviewer asks, can you tell me about

406
00:19:22,750 --> 00:19:28,430
a project you worked on, or maybe you were in a meeting, a presentation, or even a casual

407
00:19:28,430 --> 00:19:33,390
conversation with colleagues. Someone might ask you to describe something you worked on,

408
00:19:33,390 --> 00:19:37,710
and suddenly it becomes difficult to organize your thoughts in English.

409
00:19:37,710 --> 00:19:42,710
Yes, that's very true. Many English learners know their projects very well because they

410
00:19:42,710 --> 00:19:46,830
spend a lot of time working on them. But when they try to explain them in English,

411
00:19:46,830 --> 00:19:51,470
they feel nervous or confused. They are not sure where to start, what details to

412
00:19:51,470 --> 00:19:56,910
include or how to organize their explanation. The good news is that explaining a project

413
00:19:56,910 --> 00:20:02,510
does not need to be complicated. In fact, the key is simply to tell the story of the project

414
00:20:02,510 --> 00:20:08,910
clearly. I like that idea. Telling the story of the project. A project really is like a story.

415
00:20:08,910 --> 00:20:13,870
It has a beginning, a middle, and an end. When you think of it this way, explaining

416
00:20:13,870 --> 00:20:18,910
a project becomes much easier. You simply guide the listener through what happened.

417
00:20:18,910 --> 00:20:19,910
Exactly.

418
00:20:19,910 --> 00:20:25,270
Many people try to explain everything about the project, including small technical details.

419
00:20:25,270 --> 00:20:27,870
But that often makes the explanation confusing.

420
00:20:27,870 --> 00:20:31,630
Instead, it is better to focus on the most important parts.

421
00:20:31,630 --> 00:20:35,470
Think about the project as something you were sharing with someone who knows nothing about

422
00:20:35,470 --> 00:20:36,470
it.

423
00:20:36,470 --> 00:20:40,070
Your job is to help them understand what you did and why it mattered.

424
00:20:40,070 --> 00:20:42,030
That's a great way to think about it.

425
00:20:42,030 --> 00:20:46,670
Also, it's important to remember that when people ask about a project, they usually

426
00:20:46,670 --> 00:20:51,830
want to learn a few key things. They want to know what the project was about, why it

427
00:20:51,830 --> 00:20:57,270
was important, what you personally did, what difficulties you faced, and what the final

428
00:20:57,270 --> 00:20:58,270
outcome was.

429
00:20:58,270 --> 00:21:03,670
Yes, and when you explain those points clearly, the listener can easily understand the entire

430
00:21:03,670 --> 00:21:08,430
experience. Another important thing to remember is that you don't need very complicated

431
00:21:08,430 --> 00:21:13,530
English to explain a project well. Simple, clear sentences are usually much better

432
00:21:13,530 --> 00:21:15,750
than long, complex ones.

433
00:21:15,750 --> 00:21:18,750
That's a really important message for learners.

434
00:21:18,750 --> 00:21:23,410
Sometimes people think they need advanced vocabulary to sound professional, but often

435
00:21:23,410 --> 00:21:27,450
the best explanations are the ones that are simple and clear.

436
00:21:27,450 --> 00:21:31,490
If someone understands you easily, you are communicating successfully.

437
00:21:31,490 --> 00:21:32,970
Exactly.

438
00:21:32,970 --> 00:21:33,970
Communication is the goal.

439
00:21:33,970 --> 00:21:38,410
When you describe a project, you are not trying to impress people with big words.

440
00:21:38,410 --> 00:21:42,530
You are trying to explain an experience in a way that is easy to follow.

441
00:21:42,530 --> 00:21:45,210
Let's imagine a simple situation.

442
00:21:45,210 --> 00:21:49,370
Suppose someone asks you, tell me about a project you worked on recently.

443
00:21:49,370 --> 00:21:52,930
Many people might pause and feel unsure about how to begin.

444
00:21:52,930 --> 00:21:57,370
Yes, and that pause usually happens because people are trying to organize everything in

445
00:21:57,370 --> 00:21:59,050
their heads at the same time.

446
00:21:59,050 --> 00:22:04,770
They're thinking about the details, the timeline, the results, and their role in the project.

447
00:22:04,770 --> 00:22:08,450
But a much easier approach is to start with a simple introduction.

448
00:22:08,450 --> 00:22:09,450
Right.

449
00:22:09,450 --> 00:22:13,530
You can begin with a short sentence that introduces the project, something like,

450
00:22:13,530 --> 00:22:18,730
Last year, I worked on a project to improve our company's website, or maybe during my

451
00:22:18,730 --> 00:22:23,210
university studies, I worked on a research project about renewable energy.

452
00:22:23,210 --> 00:22:28,650
Those kinds of sentences immediately give the listener a clear idea of what the project was.

453
00:22:28,650 --> 00:22:33,450
Once the listener understands the general topic, they can follow the rest of the explanation

454
00:22:33,450 --> 00:22:38,490
much more easily. Another good thing about starting this way is that it gives you confidence.

455
00:22:38,490 --> 00:22:44,890
When you say the first sentence clearly, your mind begins to relax, and it becomes easier to continue speaking.

456
00:22:44,890 --> 00:22:48,390
That's very true. Many speakers struggle only at the beginning.

457
00:22:48,390 --> 00:22:52,290
Once they start talking, their ideas begin to flow more naturally.

458
00:22:52,290 --> 00:22:56,590
It's similar to starting a conversation. The first sentence is often the hardest.

459
00:22:56,590 --> 00:22:59,790
Let's talk about describing the purpose of the project.

460
00:22:59,790 --> 00:23:04,890
When people hear about a project, they naturally want to understand why it existed.

461
00:23:04,890 --> 00:23:06,890
What problem were you trying to solve?

462
00:23:06,890 --> 00:23:08,890
What improvement were you trying to make?

463
00:23:08,890 --> 00:23:12,890
Yes, and explaining the purpose gives meaning to the project.

464
00:23:12,890 --> 00:23:14,890
For example, imagine someone says,

465
00:23:14,890 --> 00:23:17,890
I worked on a project to redesign a website.

466
00:23:17,890 --> 00:23:20,890
That sentence tells us what the project was,

467
00:23:20,890 --> 00:23:23,890
but we still don't know why it was necessary.

468
00:23:23,890 --> 00:23:25,890
Exactly. But if they continue by saying,

469
00:23:25,890 --> 00:23:29,890
the goal was to make the website easier for customers to use,

470
00:23:29,890 --> 00:23:32,890
suddenly the project makes much more sense.

471
00:23:32,890 --> 00:23:34,090
That's right.

472
00:23:34,090 --> 00:23:37,410
The purpose connects the project to a real need.

473
00:23:37,410 --> 00:23:42,250
Maybe the company wanted to attract more customers, or maybe users were having difficulty navigating

474
00:23:42,250 --> 00:23:43,370
the website.

475
00:23:43,370 --> 00:23:48,530
When you explain the purpose, the listener understands the motivation behind the project.

476
00:23:48,530 --> 00:23:53,610
Now let's talk about something that is very important in professional conversations,

477
00:23:53,610 --> 00:23:55,250
describing your personal role.

478
00:23:55,250 --> 00:23:59,050
Yes, this is especially important during job interviews.

479
00:23:59,050 --> 00:24:04,330
often ask about projects because they want to understand how you contribute to a team.

480
00:24:04,330 --> 00:24:09,770
If you only say what the team did, they may not clearly see your individual contribution.

481
00:24:09,770 --> 00:24:15,050
So instead of saying, we created a new system, it can be helpful to explain your specific

482
00:24:15,050 --> 00:24:20,430
responsibility. Exactly. For example, you might say, my role in the project was to

483
00:24:20,430 --> 00:24:26,170
analyze customer feedback and identify areas for improvement. Or, I was responsible

484
00:24:26,170 --> 00:24:29,970
for coordinating communication between different departments.

485
00:24:29,970 --> 00:24:33,370
Those kinds of statements show ownership and responsibility.

486
00:24:33,370 --> 00:24:37,410
They help the listener understand what skills you used during the project.

487
00:24:37,410 --> 00:24:41,610
And it's completely normal to use the word I when talking about your role.

488
00:24:41,610 --> 00:24:45,330
Some people feel shy about doing that, but it's important because you were describing

489
00:24:45,330 --> 00:24:47,170
your own experience.

490
00:24:47,170 --> 00:24:51,850
Another interesting part of explaining a project is talking about the challenges or difficulties

491
00:24:51,850 --> 00:24:53,450
you faced along the way.

492
00:24:53,450 --> 00:24:57,170
Yes, and challenges actually make your story more interesting.

493
00:24:57,170 --> 00:24:59,530
Every project has some kind of difficulty.

494
00:24:59,530 --> 00:25:04,710
Maybe there was a tight deadline, limited resources, or unexpected technical problems.

495
00:25:04,710 --> 00:25:09,050
When you talk about challenges, you also have the opportunity to show how you solve

496
00:25:09,050 --> 00:25:10,050
problems.

497
00:25:10,050 --> 00:25:11,050
Exactly.

498
00:25:11,050 --> 00:25:15,350
For example, someone might say, one challenge we faced was that the deadline was very

499
00:25:15,350 --> 00:25:19,250
short, so we had to prioritize the most important tasks.

500
00:25:19,250 --> 00:25:24,010
That sentence shows that the team had to think carefully and make decisions under pressure.

501
00:25:24,010 --> 00:25:26,850
Another example might be communication challenges.

502
00:25:26,850 --> 00:25:32,650
In many organizations, different departments need to work together, and sometimes misunderstandings

503
00:25:32,650 --> 00:25:33,650
happen.

504
00:25:33,650 --> 00:25:38,090
Yes, and in that case, someone might explain, at the beginning of the project, communication

505
00:25:38,090 --> 00:25:42,690
between teams was difficult, so we started having weekly meetings to keep everyone

506
00:25:42,690 --> 00:25:43,690
updated.

507
00:25:43,690 --> 00:25:45,530
This shows initiative and leadership.

508
00:25:45,530 --> 00:25:50,330
I like how these examples focus on not only the problem, but also the solution.

509
00:25:50,330 --> 00:25:51,870
That's the key point.

510
00:25:51,870 --> 00:25:55,970
When you talk about challenges, always explain what action you took.

511
00:25:55,970 --> 00:25:59,650
The listener wants to see how you respond to difficult situations.

512
00:25:59,650 --> 00:26:02,750
Finally, we come to the result of the project.

513
00:26:02,750 --> 00:26:05,810
This is the moment where you explain what happened at the end.

514
00:26:05,810 --> 00:26:09,010
Yes, the result shows the impact of the work.

515
00:26:09,010 --> 00:26:14,910
Maybe the project improved efficiency, increased sales, or helped customers in some way.

516
00:26:14,910 --> 00:26:19,550
When possible, it's helpful to include numbers or measurable outcomes.

517
00:26:19,550 --> 00:26:21,790
Numbers can make the result more powerful.

518
00:26:21,790 --> 00:26:27,110
For example, someone might say, after launching the new website, online sales increased by

519
00:26:27,110 --> 00:26:28,710
15%.

520
00:26:28,710 --> 00:26:32,030
That kind of information gives a clear picture of success.

521
00:26:32,030 --> 00:26:36,430
Even if you don't have numbers, you can still describe the outcome in a meaningful way.

522
00:26:36,430 --> 00:26:41,630
For example, you might say the project helped improve communication between departments,

523
00:26:41,630 --> 00:26:44,950
Or we received very positive feedback from users.

524
00:26:44,950 --> 00:26:49,290
At this point, the listener understands the entire journey of the project.

525
00:26:49,290 --> 00:26:54,190
How it started, what you did, what challenges appeared, and what happened in the end.

526
00:26:54,190 --> 00:26:55,190
Exactly.

527
00:26:55,190 --> 00:26:58,910
And when you practice explaining projects this way, you become more confident each

528
00:26:58,910 --> 00:27:00,190
time you do it.

529
00:27:00,190 --> 00:27:03,470
Over time, it becomes a natural part of your communication.

530
00:27:03,470 --> 00:27:07,750
Another useful tip is to practice explaining your projects out loud.

531
00:27:07,750 --> 00:27:12,870
Many learners only think about the explanation in their minds, but speaking out loud helps

532
00:27:12,870 --> 00:27:15,590
you organize your ideas more clearly.

533
00:27:15,590 --> 00:27:16,750
That's a great suggestion.

534
00:27:16,750 --> 00:27:20,550
You can practice alone, record yourself, or practice with a friend.

535
00:27:20,550 --> 00:27:25,190
The goal is simply to become comfortable describing your experiences in English.

536
00:27:25,190 --> 00:27:29,990
You might even practice explaining different kinds of projects, a work project, a school

537
00:27:29,990 --> 00:27:32,470
project, or a personal project.

538
00:27:32,470 --> 00:27:35,510
Yes, personal projects are great examples too.

539
00:27:35,510 --> 00:27:40,990
Maybe you organized an event, created a website, started a small business, or learned a new

540
00:27:40,990 --> 00:27:41,990
skill.

541
00:27:41,990 --> 00:27:45,950
All of these experiences can be explained using the same storytelling approach.

542
00:27:45,950 --> 00:27:51,150
And the more you practice telling these stories, the easier it becomes to speak naturally during

543
00:27:51,150 --> 00:27:52,590
real conversations.

544
00:27:52,590 --> 00:27:53,990
That's true.

545
00:27:53,990 --> 00:27:58,270
Practice builds confidence, and confidence makes communication smoother.

546
00:27:58,270 --> 00:28:03,030
Before we finish today's episode, I want to remind our listeners of one very important

547
00:28:03,030 --> 00:28:04,030
idea.

548
00:28:04,030 --> 00:28:08,230
When you explain a project in English, your goal is not perfection.

549
00:28:08,230 --> 00:28:09,750
Your goal is clarity.

550
00:28:09,750 --> 00:28:10,750
Exactly.

551
00:28:10,750 --> 00:28:14,190
You don't need perfect grammar or advanced vocabulary.

552
00:28:14,190 --> 00:28:19,670
What matters most is that the listener understands your story and the value of the work you did.

553
00:28:19,670 --> 00:28:25,870
So remember to stay calm, organize your thoughts, and explain your experience step by step.

554
00:28:25,870 --> 00:28:28,550
And most importantly, keep practicing.

555
00:28:28,550 --> 00:28:32,310
Every time you talk about a project, you become a stronger communicator.

556
00:28:32,310 --> 00:28:34,310
Thank you so much for joining us today.

557
00:28:34,310 --> 00:28:39,230
We hope this conversation helps you feel more confident the next time someone asks you about

558
00:28:39,230 --> 00:28:40,630
a project you worked on.

559
00:28:40,630 --> 00:28:44,890
Yes, thank you for listening, and we look forward to sharing another English-learning conversation

560
00:28:44,890 --> 00:28:45,890
with you soon.

561
00:28:45,890 --> 00:28:50,510
Until next time, keep practicing, keep learning, and keep speaking English.

